WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.959
The capstone circuit lesson becomes much safer when the learner stops hunting for one magic rule that applies everywhere at once.

2
00:00:05.959 --> 00:00:11.918
A mixed network contains different local structures.

3
00:00:11.918 --> 00:00:17.877
Some components sit on one uninterrupted path and share one current story.

4
00:00:17.877 --> 00:00:23.836
Other components sit across the same two junctions and share one voltage story.

5
00:00:23.836 --> 00:00:29.795
The safest opening move is structural, not numerical.

6
00:00:29.795 --> 00:00:35.755
Identify the valid block first, reduce that block, then rebuild the source and branch quantities in order.

7
00:00:35.755 --> 00:00:41.714
Keep this takeaway in view: Lesson 6 becomes safer once structure is read before equations are applied.

8
00:00:41.714 --> 00:00:47.673
Series rule means same current on one path.

9
00:00:47.673 --> 00:00:53.632
Use the same-current story only where the route is truly one uninterrupted chain.

10
00:00:53.632 --> 00:00:59.591
Parallel rule means same voltage across shared junctions.

11
00:00:59.591 --> 00:01:05.550
Use the same-voltage story only where the components connect across the same two junctions.

12
00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:13.135
This diagram matters because it turns connection logic into something the learner can point at.

13
00:01:13.135 --> 00:01:20.720
A branch block is parallel only because both components share the same two junctions.

14
00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:28.305
A series section is series only because the current has one uninterrupted route there.

15
00:01:28.305 --> 00:01:35.890
Once that logic is visible, the first reduction step stops feeling arbitrary.

16
00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:43.475
The learner can say which block is valid before any arithmetic appears, and that is exactly the habit this mixed-circuit lesson needs.

17
00:01:43.475 --> 00:01:51.060
Keep this takeaway in view: A strong mixed-circuit answer starts by naming the valid block before touching the numbers.

18
00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:58.645
Shared junctions: These reveal the branch block that must be treated as parallel.

19
00:01:58.645 --> 00:02:06.230
One-path section: This outer chain keeps the same source current because the route there does not split.

20
00:02:06.230 --> 00:02:12.075
A road-network analogy helps because it shows why one global slogan is too weak for a mixed circuit.

21
00:02:12.075 --> 00:02:17.921
On the trunk road, every van follows the same route.

22
00:02:17.921 --> 00:02:23.766
At the split, the flow can divide between two side streets.

23
00:02:23.766 --> 00:02:29.612
At the rejoin, the totals rebuild.

24
00:02:29.612 --> 00:02:35.457
The strongest lesson habit is to describe the split and rejoin before calculating anything.

25
00:02:35.457 --> 00:02:41.303
That is the bridge into mixed circuits: same-current language belongs on the trunk, same-voltage branch language belongs across the split section, and the whole problem is solved in stages.

26
00:02:41.303 --> 00:02:47.148
Keep this takeaway in view: The analogy makes local rules feel natural instead of arbitrary.

27
00:02:47.148 --> 00:02:52.994
Trunk road means same flow.

28
00:02:52.994 --> 00:02:58.839
One uninterrupted route matches the series section where the same current continues.

29
00:02:58.839 --> 00:03:04.685
Side streets means shared endpoints.

30
00:03:04.685 --> 00:03:10.530
The branches begin and end at the same junctions, matching the parallel block.

31
00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:17.381
The first key rule is that series resistances add directly because the same current passes each resistor on that one path.

32
00:03:17.381 --> 00:03:24.232
The second key rule is that parallel resistances combine through the reciprocal relation because the same voltage sits across each branch while the current can split.

33
00:03:24.232 --> 00:03:31.083
The lesson stays rich when the learner sees those as local rules.

34
00:03:31.083 --> 00:03:37.934
After a valid block is reduced, Ohm's law can then be used on the total circuit and on the branch sections in the correct order.

35
00:03:37.934 --> 00:03:44.785
Keep this takeaway in view: The formula scene becomes safe once each rule is tied to the local structure that justifies it.

36
00:03:44.785 --> 00:03:51.635
Series use: Add resistances directly only on the true one-path section.

37
00:03:51.635 --> 00:03:58.486
Parallel use: Use the reciprocal rule only on the components that share the same two junctions.

38
00:03:58.486 --> 00:04:05.337
R_series = R1 + R2 + ...

39
00:04:05.337 --> 00:04:12.188
1 / R_parallel = 1 / R1 + 1 / R2 + ...

40
00:04:12.188 --> 00:04:19.039
Reduce the valid local block first.

41
00:04:19.039 --> 00:04:25.890
Then rebuild the total current and the branch values in order.

42
00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:32.058
A strong routine for this lesson has three steps.

43
00:04:32.058 --> 00:04:38.226
First identify the true series section and the true parallel block.

44
00:04:38.226 --> 00:04:44.394
Second reduce the valid local block into one equivalent resistance.

45
00:04:44.394 --> 00:04:50.562
Third rebuild the whole circuit from the source, then return to the branches only where the current or voltage rule actually applies.

46
00:04:50.562 --> 00:04:56.730
This sequence protects the learner from forcing one current rule or one voltage rule across the whole network before the structure has been simplified honestly.

47
00:04:56.730 --> 00:05:02.898
Keep this takeaway in view: Identify, reduce, and rebuild is the safest routine for every mixed-circuit problem.

48
00:05:02.898 --> 00:05:09.066
Step 1 means identify the valid block.

49
00:05:09.066 --> 00:05:15.234
Look for one uninterrupted path and for components that share the same two junctions.

50
00:05:15.234 --> 00:05:21.402
Step 2 means reduce the local block.

51
00:05:21.402 --> 00:05:27.570
Simplify the valid series or parallel section before touching the source current.

52
00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:33.363
The same-current rule is powerful, but only in the place where it is actually true.

53
00:05:33.363 --> 00:05:39.156
If there is one uninterrupted route, the same charge flow rate must pass each component on that chain.

54
00:05:39.156 --> 00:05:44.949
But the moment a junction appears, the current can split between branches.

55
00:05:44.949 --> 00:05:50.742
That is why a mixed-circuit answer must point to the one-path section before using the same-current sentence.

56
00:05:50.742 --> 00:05:56.535
The rule is local, not global.

57
00:05:56.535 --> 00:06:02.328
Keep this takeaway in view: The same-current rule is strong only when the learner first proves the route is one uninterrupted path.

58
00:06:02.328 --> 00:06:08.121
One path means same current.

59
00:06:08.121 --> 00:06:13.914
A single uninterrupted route means the same current passes each point on that chain.

60
00:06:13.914 --> 00:06:19.707
Junction appears means current can split.

61
00:06:19.707 --> 00:06:25.500
Once the route branches, different branch currents can exist.

62
00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:31.361
The parallel-voltage rule is also local.

63
00:06:31.361 --> 00:06:37.222
Branches share the same potential difference only because they connect across the same pair of junctions.

64
00:06:37.222 --> 00:06:43.083
That rule does not automatically apply to the outer series resistor or to every component in the whole network.

65
00:06:43.083 --> 00:06:48.944
The safest habit is to identify the two junctions, then say which components sit directly across them.

66
00:06:48.944 --> 00:06:54.805
Once that is done, the branch-voltage story becomes trustworthy.

67
00:06:54.805 --> 00:07:00.666
Keep this takeaway in view: The same-voltage rule stays honest only when it is tied to the shared-junction block.

68
00:07:00.666 --> 00:07:06.527
Shared pair means same voltage.

69
00:07:06.527 --> 00:07:12.388
Components across the same two junctions share the same potential difference.

70
00:07:12.388 --> 00:07:18.249
Outside the block means different role.

71
00:07:18.249 --> 00:07:24.110
A series resistor outside the branch block does not automatically share that same voltage.

72
00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:29.966
Reduction order is where the mixed-circuit lesson either becomes elegant or collapses.

73
00:07:29.966 --> 00:07:35.823
Start with the valid parallel block and replace it with one equivalent resistance.

74
00:07:35.823 --> 00:07:41.679
Then add that equivalent resistance to the true series resistor to get the total resistance.

75
00:07:41.679 --> 00:07:47.535
Only after that should the source current be found.

76
00:07:47.535 --> 00:07:53.392
From there, the voltage across the branch block can be recovered, and finally each branch current can be calculated.

77
00:07:53.392 --> 00:07:59.248
The sequence matters because each later number depends on the earlier structural step being correct.

78
00:07:59.248 --> 00:08:05.105
Keep this takeaway in view: The capstone becomes manageable when the learner respects the reduction order instead of improvising it.

79
00:08:05.105 --> 00:08:10.961
Block first means find R_parallel.

80
00:08:10.961 --> 00:08:16.817
Reduce the branch pair before you add anything in the outer chain.

81
00:08:16.817 --> 00:08:22.674
Total second means find R_total then I_source.

82
00:08:22.674 --> 00:08:28.530
Use the reduced block to get the full resistance and the source current.

83
00:08:28.530 --> 00:08:33.115
Run four checks.

84
00:08:33.115 --> 00:08:37.699
Identify the valid branch block from the shared junctions.

85
00:08:37.699 --> 00:08:42.284
Reduce that local block before adding the series resistor.

86
00:08:42.284 --> 00:08:46.868
Use the total resistance to find the source current.

87
00:08:46.868 --> 00:08:51.453
Then return to the branches with the correct branch-voltage or branch-current rule.

88
00:08:51.453 --> 00:08:56.037
Those four checks capture the whole lesson structure before the arithmetic begins.

89
00:08:56.037 --> 00:09:00.622
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready once the learner can explain the reduction order as well as the formulas.

90
00:09:00.622 --> 00:09:05.206
Structure means find the valid block.

91
00:09:05.206 --> 00:09:09.791
Shared junctions reveal the parallel section that should be reduced first.

92
00:09:09.791 --> 00:09:14.375
Reduction means local before global.

93
00:09:14.375 --> 00:09:18.960
Simplify the branch block before calculating the total current.

94
00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:25.180
Start by identifying the parallel pair and finding its equivalent resistance.

95
00:09:25.180 --> 00:09:31.400
Then add the outer series resistor to get the total resistance and use the source voltage to find the source current.

96
00:09:31.400 --> 00:09:37.620
After that, recover the voltage across the branch block and use it to find each branch current.

97
00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:43.840
Finish by checking that the branch currents add back to the source current.

98
00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:50.060
That is the cleanest route into the worked example.

99
00:09:50.060 --> 00:09:56.280
Keep this takeaway in view: Lesson 6 becomes much safer once reduction order feels like the main idea rather than an afterthought.

100
00:09:56.280 --> 00:10:02.500
Do not add blindly: Check which components are truly series or truly parallel before combining resistances.

101
00:10:02.500 --> 00:10:08.720
Do not skip the rebuild: Use the reduced total circuit to get the source current before returning to the branches.

102
00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:14.940
reduce the valid parallel block first.

103
00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:21.160
rebuild the total resistance and source current second.

104
00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:27.380
The best answers justify the reduction order before they celebrate the numbers.
